**Known wrinkle:** the partial-escape test uses chunks `[`{"summary": "hello \\`, `u00ff world", ...`]`. When concatenated, the buffer becomes `{"summary": "hello \u00ff world", ...`. At the first-chunk boundary, the buffer is `{"summary": "hello \` (a trailing backslash, mid-escape). `SUMMARY_RE` matches up to `hello \` — the partial is `hello \`. `JSON.parse(`"hello \"`)` throws (unterminated escape); `extractPartialSummary` returns undefined. Then `onPartial` is NOT called. After the second chunk, buffer is `{"summary": "hello \u00ff world", ...` — partial extracts as `"hello ÿ world"`, and `onPartial` fires with the full unescaped summary. This test verifies no `onPartial` ever has a summary ending in `\`.

**Real failure mode to watch:** if the regex pattern changes or the unescape logic stops rejecting partials correctly, you might see `onPartial` fire with an unterminated escape. The test has the assertion `!p.summary.endsWith("\\")` to guard that.

